Autodisk is finally done!  To use, simply cd to the directory containing the 
autodisk script and type
	autodisk pcXX 
where XX is the number of the pc to kickstart.  If you are running this from 
sp3, the machine you wish to kickstart should be in sp3's ssh known_hosts file 
so you don't have to type in the root password several times.  Once the machine
is kickstarted, it is wise to install sp3's host key on the kickstarted machine
using Andrew's Update_SSH script in ~sprog/bin.  If you don't, you will get an
hostkey change error, which you then will have to edit the known_hosts file anyway,
so it's better off to just update the host key.

When you run autodisk, there are 2 possibilities.  The first is that the custom 
image file ksimage does not exist.  In this case, autodisk calls mkFinal to create
the ksimage file.  This is a non-trivial process since there is tons of mounting,
decompressing, manipulating, recompressing, unmounting,and even filesystem creation 
going on.  Beware of any errors, for the mkFinal script continues execution on error,
and you might miss it.  

The other possibility is that the ksimage file already exists, and if so, then all that
is needed is to copy the new kickstart config file (/tmp/ks.cfg) to the ksimage.  This is
done by calling copyKS in autodisk.

Now at this point, you will see a message like "ready to deploy payload."  This is the 
point of no return.  If you press CTRL-C, you can abort the kickstart.  Otherwise, 
pressing any other key will copy install files to the destination machine, modify
lilo and /dev/hda1, and reboot which will start the kickstart process.  If for some reason
the kickstart fails between the copying of the files and rebooting, you may have to just
kickstart the old-fashioned way, or see me and I might be able to restore the machine and 
maybe find out what went wrong.

Note that autodisk does no checking to see if someone is logged in before kickstarting, so
do this on your own.  It should also be easy for anyone to modify autodisk to be used in
shell scripting (ie removing the keypress before kickstarting), or kickstart a select group
of machines. One final note - I added the commands to install the NVIDIA graphics driver
and reboot the machine in the %post section of the post_install file, so we should not have
to type those things manually like we used to do after a kickstart.

Enjoy!
-Dustin

Files:
  autodisk - The main script.  Just like disk but better. Takes pc name as arg.
             It is basically a modified version of the original disk script.
	     
  mkFinal - Script to hack the boot and driver images and compose
            them together into one superimage.  Takes the boot image
	    file, ie rh90_image, and the ks.cfg file as arguments. Produces
	    the ksimage superimage as output.  Presumes the existence of the 
	    rh90_image, drvnet.img, and the syslinux.cfg.template file in the 
	    same directory.  There are several versioning dependencies that will have
	    to be handled here when Redhat's version changes.
	    
  copyKS -  Modifies the given ksimage file by copying the given ks.cfg file to
            the ksimage, replacing the old ks.cfg already inside the ksimage.
	    
  		    
  deploy -  Script that actually installs the ksimage into the machine to be kickstarted.
    	    Depends on ks_lilo.conf being in the machine's /tmp directory.
  
  clean  -  Script that cleans some of the mess left behind by mkFinal.  It is called by
            mkFinal just before it makes the ksimage.

  README - This file.	    
  
Config/Data Files:

  ks_lilo.conf - Special version of lilo.conf that will boot the /dev/hda1 device, which is 
  	         where deploy writes ksimage.

  syslinux.cfg.template - The custom syslinux.cfg file that has the elusive parameter
                          "append ks=hd:hda1:/ks.cfg initrd=initrd.img" that tells syslinux to
			  tell the kernel that the ks.cfg file is on the hard disk.  I think this 
			  may be undocumented, I had to find it in the anaconda source code.
  
  rh90_image - The standard kickstart boot image (the one we use to copy onto floppies with the 
               old disk command). Used by mkFinal to create the custom boot/driver image.
	       
  drvnet.img - The network drivers image.  This is the image we used to copy onto floppies and label
               "driver disk".  Used by mkFinal to create the custom boot/driver image.
	       
  kickstart - From the original disk package.  Used by autodisk.  I think it is a kickstart config file
              used as a template.  It is appended to the ks.cfg file.  

  ks_environment.sh - From the original disk package.  Used by autodisk.  Must be part of the kickstart 
                       configuration, since it is appended to ks.cfg.
  
  post_install - From the original disk package.  Used by autodisk, and has something to do with the 
                 kickstart configuration since it is appended to ks.cfg. PS - I modified the %post section
	         so that the NVIDIA graphics driver would be installed, and the machine rebooted.		       
                  
				  
